From: Hans Christian Winther-Sorensen <hc@wsh.no>
Subject: [PATCH] sparc64: give the Solaris 2.5.1/2.6 boot block
 init-program for code at load-base, and ?go

The sun4u boot block of Solaris 2.5.1 and 2.6 (FCode UFS Reader 1.6 and
1.10, on slice f of the CDs) reads ufsboot into a buffer of its own,
copies its ELF segments to their addresses itself, and then evaluates
"to load-base init-program" with load-base set to the entry point,
followed by "?go". OpenBIOS's init-program only knows images with a
header (ELF, a.out, FCode, Forth), so it set nothing up, and there was
no "?go" at all: the boot block stopped with "?go:" and "byte-load:
exception caught!" before ufsboot ran. The boot block of Solaris 7 and
later hands init-program the ELF image itself and isn't affected.

Redefine init-program in the sparc64 dictionary for callers that look it
up by name (the copy that "load" calls is compiled in already and stays
as it was): clear state-valid, which a client started with "go" (the
boot block itself) left set, let the loaders look at load-base as
before, and if none of them recognised it, take it as code that starts
at load-base, as OBP does. Add "?go", which starts the program if
init-program set one up.
---
 arch/sparc64/boot.c     |  9 +++++++++
 arch/sparc64/boot.h     |  1 +
 arch/sparc64/init.fs    | 24 ++++++++++++++++++++++++
 arch/sparc64/openbios.c |  1 +
 4 files changed, 35 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/sparc64/boot.c b/arch/sparc64/boot.c
index 2534f13..031346b 100644
--- a/arch/sparc64/boot.c
+++ b/arch/sparc64/boot.c
@@ -21,6 +21,15 @@ char *boot_device;
 
 extern int sparc64_of_client_interface( int *params );
 
+/* init-program for code that a client program has put in memory itself,
+   starting at load-base (the Solaris 2.5.1 and 2.6 boot block, see init.fs) */
+void init_program_at_load_base(void)
+{
+	feval("load-base load-state >ls.entry !");
+	arch_init_program();
+	feval("-1 state-valid !");
+}
+
 /* ( path len -- path len ) */
 
 void boot(void)
diff --git a/arch/sparc64/boot.h b/arch/sparc64/boot.h
index 84a6db1..6e7e0f1 100644
--- a/arch/sparc64/boot.h
+++ b/arch/sparc64/boot.h
@@ -21,6 +21,7 @@ extern uint64_t qemu_cmdline;
 extern uint64_t cmdline_size;
 extern char *boot_device;
 extern void boot(void);
+extern void init_program_at_load_base(void);
 
 // sys_info.c
 extern uint64_t qemu_mem_size;
diff --git a/arch/sparc64/init.fs b/arch/sparc64/init.fs
index 2ffcf5a..1dae9a6 100644
--- a/arch/sparc64/init.fs
+++ b/arch/sparc64/init.fs
@@ -60,3 +60,27 @@ device-end
   -1 value vga-driver-fcode
   " QEMU,VGA.bin" $encode-file to vga-driver-fcode
 [THEN]
+
+\ The boot block of Solaris 2.5.1 and 2.6 for sun4u (FCode UFS Reader 1.6
+\ and 1.10) reads ufsboot to a buffer of its own, copies its ELF segments
+\ to their addresses itself, and then evaluates "to load-base init-program"
+\ with load-base set to the entry point, and "?go". The firmware's loaders
+\ recognise none of that code, and OpenBIOS had no "?go", so the boot block
+\ stopped with "?go:" and "byte-load: exception caught!". (The boot block of
+\ Solaris 7 and later hands init-program the whole ELF image instead.)
+\
+\ init-program by name (what "load" calls is bound already and unchanged):
+\ what the loaders recognise at load-base as before, otherwise code that
+\ starts at load-base, as OBP does. state-valid is cleared first: a client
+\ running from "go" (the boot block itself) left it set.
+: init-program ( -- )
+  0 state-valid !
+  init-program
+  state-valid @ 0= if
+    \ bound by openbios.c
+    s" (init-program-at-load-base)" $find if execute else 2drop then
+  then
+;
+
+\ Start the program that init-program set up, if there is one
+: ?go ( -- ) state-valid @ if go then ;
diff --git a/arch/sparc64/openbios.c b/arch/sparc64/openbios.c
index 55bb3bb..af51d3a 100644
--- a/arch/sparc64/openbios.c
+++ b/arch/sparc64/openbios.c
@@ -850,6 +850,7 @@ arch_init( void )
         fword("eval");
 
 	bind_func("platform-boot", boot );
+	bind_func("(init-program-at-load-base)", init_program_at_load_base );
 }
 
 unsigned long isa_io_base;
